{
  "package": "reflect",
  "notes": "Rust has no run-time reflection, so reflect is opt-in: the Reflect trait (derived by rust-stdlib-derive, implemented here for the standard types) gives each Rust type a Go type descriptor and copies values in and out of a dynamic, aliasing store. Type is an interned handle (equal exactly when Go types are identical), with Go's type strings, struct tags, method sets, assignability, convertibility and amd64 layout. Value implements Go's addressability and CanSet rules, calls, Convert (Go's numeric wraparound and string rules), maps, slices, channels and select. Methods panic where Go's do (ValueError payloads). Values are copies: reflect::edit is the idiom for ValueOf(&x).Elem().Set(...). Option maps to a pointer; Box/Rc/Arc are transparent; tuples map to structs. Float to integer Convert is exact where Go defines it (in range); out-of-range and NaN results are implementation-defined in Go and follow Rust saturation here. Interop: fmt::Value and text/template::Value are built from reflect::Value (From impls); encoding/json keeps its typed Serialize path and has no conversion.",
